    "https://www.jobs.ac.uk/job/DTD267/senior-research-technician": {
      "title": "Senior Research Technician",
      "description": "Senior Research Technician at University of Warwick Skip to main content Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job Recruiters Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice Jobs by Email Advertise a Job Your Account Recruiters Back to search results Senior Research Technician University of Warwick - School of Life Sciences Location: Coventry, University of Warwick Salary: £31,236 to £34,610\n                                                                                        per annum. Hours: Part Time Contract Type: Fixed-Term/Contract Placed On: 2nd October 2026 Closes: 25th October 2026 Job Ref: 357 Closing Date: Sunday, 25 October 2026, 11:59pm (UK). About the Role Applications are invited for a Research Technician position to work on a NERC-funded project titled “Partners in crime: Does microbial cooperation exacerbate nitrous oxide emission from soil?”. The aim of the project is to investigate understand how microbial interactions influence nitrogen cycling and N2O production from soil. About the research group: You will join the Lehtovirta-Morley research group in the School of Life Sciences at Warwick University. We are a diverse group of interdisciplinary scientists who study the ecology, physiology and biochemistry of ammonia oxidation. For more information about our lab visit: Lehtovirta-Morley lab, Warwick: https://www.lehtovirta-morleylab.com/ . Any requests for further information about the role can be directed to Prof. Laura Lehtovirta-Morley ( laura.lehtovirta-morley@warwick.ac.uk ). Please note this is an on-campus based role due to the nature of the work, therefore we cannot accommodate any work from home basis. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description ( link here ). About You We are looking for someone with expertise in microbiology and molecular biology but we also welcome applications from people with laboratory management tasks. Applicants should be proficient in planning and undertaking of experiments, handling microorganisms, and working in a laboratory environment. The successful applicant will provide essential technical research support for this NERC grant on microbial cooperation in soil ecosystems. The key responsibilities include the cultivation of ammonia-oxidising and heterotrophic microorganisms, analysis of inorganic nitrogen, molecular techniques (DNA extraction, PCR, cloning) and analysis of soil microcosms. The successful applicant will have experience of working in an environmental microbiology or molecular microbiology laboratory. In addition, you will be able to satisfy all essential criteria in the person specification for this post. The position is available from the 1st of December 2026 for 23 months on a 75% FTE basis. About the Department School of Life Sciences For further information about the School of Life Sciences please see our website. We believe that collegiality, openness, inclusivity and diversity are essential for an excellent research and teaching environment. Our workplace ethic has enabled us to gain the Athena SWAN Silver award. We are dedicated to building a diverse and inclusive workplace and encourage applications from all groups with protected characteristics traditionally under-represented in STEM fields. As part of our ongoing commitment to addressing gender equality under the Athena Swan charter mark, we would particularly encourage and welcome applications from female academics. The School of Life Sciences (SLS) has world-leading research in microbiology including environmental microbiology and biogeochemical cycling, plant-microbe interactions, microbial biochemistry and metabolism, and structural microbiology. All across SLS our research is supported by cutting-edge experimental and computational facilities, including genomics, proteomics, high-performance computing, advanced confocal, flow cytometry, light and electron microscopy facilities and bioinformatics. We and the wider University of Warwick environment offer substantial opportunities for professional development including regular opportunities to attend international conferences. Full details of the duties and selection criteria for this role can be found in the vacancy advert on the University of Warwick's jobs pages. You will be routed to this when you click on the 'Apply' button. Share on social media We value your feedback on the quality of our adverts.\n            If you have a comment to make about the overall quality of this advert,\n            or its categorisation then please send us your feedback Advert information Type / Role: Subject Area(s): Location(s): Job tools PhD Alert Created Job Alert Created Your PhD alert has been successfully created for this search. Your job alert has been successfully created for this search. Ok Ok PhD Alert Created Job Alert Created Your PhD alert has been successfully created for this search. Your job alert has been successfully created for this search. Manage your job alerts Manage your job alerts Account Verification Missing In order to create multiple job alerts, you must first verify your email address to complete your account creation Request verification email Request verification email jobs.ac.uk Account Required In order to create multiple alerts, you must create a jobs.ac.uk jobseeker account Create Account Create Account Alert Creation Failed Unfortunately, your account is currently blocked. Please login to unblock your account. Login Login Email Address Blocked We received a delivery failure message when attempting to send you an email and therefore your email address has been blocked. You will not receive job alerts until your email address is unblocked. To do so, please choose from one of the two options below. Re-verify your email Update your email Re-verify your email Update your email Max Alerts Reached A maximum of 5 Job Alerts can be created against your account. Please remove an existing alert in order to create this new Job Alert Manage your job alerts Manage your job alerts Creation Failed Unfortunately, your alert was not created at this time. Please try again. Ok Ok Create PhD Alert Create Job Alert When you create this PhD alert we will email you a selection of PhDs matching your criteria. When you create this job alert we will email you a selection of jobs matching your criteria. Our Terms and Conditions and Privacy Policy apply to this service. Any personal data you provide in setting up this alert is processed in accordance with our Privacy Notice Create PhD Alert Create Job Alert When you create this PhD alert we will email you a selection of PhDs matching your criteria. When you create this job alert we will email you a selection of jobs matching your criteria. Our Terms and Conditions and Privacy Policy apply to this service. Any personal data you provide in setting up this alert is processed in accordance with our Privacy Notice I would like to receive email communication from jobs.ac.uk relating to Company News I would like to receive email communication from jobs.ac.uk relating to Conferences, Meetings and Events Save this job Login to Save Job Max Saved Jobs Reached × Max Saved Jobs Reached A maximum of 500 Saved Jobs can be created against your account. Please remove an existing Saved Job in order to add a new Saved Job. Manage Saved Jobs × Save Job Please sign in or register for an account to save a job. Sign In Register More jobs from University of Warwick Early Years Practitioner (Nursery Assistant) Mechanical Maintenance Manager Assistant Campus Cleaning Services Manager Associate Professor (Human-AI Teaming) Research Fellow Assistant Clinical Professor Show all jobs for this employer … More jobs like this Lecturer in Pathobiology Prize Fellow Lecturer in Microbiology (0.6 FTE) Research Associate in Modelling Friedreich's Ataxia Using iPSC-derived Neurons Research Scientist - T Cell immunology Join in and follow us Copyright © jobs.ac.uk 1998 - 2026 Find a 