    "https://www.jobs.ac.uk/job/DTE751/marie-curie-early-stage-researcher": {
      "title": "Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher",
      "description": "Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher at University of Warwick Skip to main content Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice PhDs by Email Advertise a Job Recruiters Find a Job Find PhDs Career Advice PhDs by Email Advertise a Job Your Account Recruiters Back to search results Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher University of Warwick - School of Engineering Qualification Type: PhD Location: Coventry, University of Warwick Funding for: UK Students, EU Students, International Students Funding amount: £39,600\n                                                                                        per annum (specific salary as per Marie Curie rate) Hours: Full Time Placed On: 7th October 2026 Closes: 1st November 2026 Reference: 383 About the Role Please note: the salary advertised covers a range of allowances and pension enrolments. Final salary will be dependent on entitlement to these allowances. The School of Engineering is looking to appoint a Early Stage Marie Curie Researcher to undertake high-quality research within the MSCA Doctoral Network POSEIDON ( https://poseidon-dn.eu/ ). The postholder will be assisting in developing the next generation of ecofriendly offshore windfarms and closely collaborating with academic and industrial partners across the EU to deliver the scientific and training objectives of the project. The successful candidate will undertake innovative research focused on addressing key challenges in sustainable offshore wind development, contributing to improved understanding, design and performance of offshore wind infrastructure and its interaction with the marine environment. They will also have opportunities to develop their research skills through collaboration across the POSEIDON network, international secondments and dedicated training activities. This post will have heavy emphasis on data science & ML programming applied to large environmental datasets (geospatial, weather, tide). This is an excellent opportunity for an early-career researcher to gain international research experience, develop a strong academic and industrial network, and contribute to the transition towards more sustainable offshore renewable energy. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description ( link here ). About You A MSc or equivalent in Civil/Geotechnical Engineering, Ocean/Offshore Engineering, Applied Mechanics, Naval Architecture, or a closely related discipline You should evidence the ability to deliver independent research, and strong potential for high-quality research, with a developing profile and an understanding of relevant methods and techniques. Strong numerical and computational skills, plus proficiency in at least one of: Python, MATLAB, or C++, particularly for data analysis, automation, or machine learning implementation is essential, as is previous experience with computational mechanics and laboratory and physical tests for soil characterisation. You will be a collaborative and motivated individual, able to work effectively with partners across Europe and willing to undertake international secondments. Strong communication skills (in English), excellent IT proficiency, and the ability to deliver work on time are essential. Candidates will be required to meet Marie Curie Early Stage Researcher eligibility criteria which includes: At, applicants must not have been awarded a PhD. Candidates should not have lived or carried out their main activity in the UK for more than 12 months in the 3 years immediately before their recruitment. Applicants must be in the first four years (full-time equivalent research experience) of their research career You will be registered for a PhD degree at the University of Warwick. Full details of the duties and selection criteria for this role can be found in the vacancy advert on the University of Warwick's jobs pages. You will be routed to this when you click on the 'Apply' button. Share on social media We value your feedback 